<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AN UNRESOLVABLE REFERENCE MUST COME BACK OUT EXACTLY AS IT WENT IN.

  A MISSING placeholder re-serializes the spelling the PROJECT FILE used,
  untouched — it does NOT re-encode it against this machine's anchor. That is
  not a nicety: the reference is unresolvable HERE, so this machine has no
  standing to rewrite where it points, and a project carried back to the machine
  where the file DOES live would arrive broken by the mere act of having been
  opened somewhere else. The failure is silent, permanent and one save away.

  WHY THIS CASE NEEDS ITS OWN FIXTURE, and why the obvious one cannot gate it.
  collect_external_media_missing.qxa also re-saves a placeholder, but its
  reference is `~/__smaragd_no_such_dir_missing__/...` — a HOME-relative form
  whose re-encoding produces the identical string, because the path climbs to
  home either way. So that assertion passes whether the rule holds or not. This
  fixture uses a PROJECT-RELATIVE reference to a file that does not exist
  (`samples/__missing_rel__.wav`) and then saves the project THREE LEVELS AWAY,
  which is the one shape where the two answers differ visibly:

    verbatim (correct) : samples/__missing_rel__.wav
    re-encoded (wrong) : an anchor-relative climb built out of the working
                         directory the app happened to be run from — a path
                         that names nothing on any machine, including this one.

  Verified by sabotage: replacing the verbatim branch with a re-encode leaves
  collect_external_media_missing.qxa GREEN and fails this case.

  The good clip is here for the same reason it is in every case in this family:
  it proves the load did not merely fail quietly.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="missing_sample_reference_verbatim">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../missing_relative_sample.qxp"/>

    <!-- Both waves present; one of them a placeholder. -->
    <assert-extern-files count="2" missing="1"/>

    <!-- Saved three levels away, so a re-encode cannot coincide with the
         original spelling. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/missing_sample_reference_verbatim/song.qxp"/>

    <assert-file-contains path="../../build/test-output/missing_sample_reference_verbatim/song.qxp"
                          text="filename='samples/__missing_rel__.wav'"/>

    <!-- The surviving clip is real audio: source sec0 of the ramped sawtooth. -->
    <render filename="missing_sample_reference_verbatim.wav" format="wav"
            quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="missing_sample_reference_verbatim.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.05" maxRms="0.085"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
